narrative:
## A Time Conversion Toolkit

Time units follow fixed ratios: 60 seconds per minute, 60 minutes per hour, 24 hours per day. These constants make time functions predictable and testable.

### Small to Large

```xiom
fn minutes_to_seconds(minutes: Int) -> Int {
  return minutes * 60;
}

fn hours_to_minutes(hours: Int) -> Int {
  return hours * 60;
}

fn days_to_hours(days: Int) -> Int {
  return days * 24;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(minutes_to_seconds(5));   // 300
  io.println(hours_to_minutes(2));      // 120
  io.println(days_to_hours(3));         // 72
  return 0;
}
```

### Large to Small (Reverse)

```xiom
fn seconds_to_minutes(seconds: Int) -> Int {
  return seconds / 60;
}

fn minutes_to_hours(minutes: Int) -> Int {
  return minutes / 60;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(seconds_to_minutes(180));  // 3
  io.println(minutes_to_hours(150));    // 2
  return 0;
}
```

### Duration Description

```xiom
fn describe_duration(minutes: Int) {
  let seconds = minutes_to_seconds(minutes);
  let hours = minutes_to_hours(minutes);
  io.println("Minutes: ");
  io.println(minutes);
  io.println("Seconds: ");
  io.println(seconds);
  io.println("Hours (approx): ");
  io.println(hours);
}

fn main() -> Int {
  describe_duration(90);
  return 0;
}
```

### Why Use Functions for This?

You could write `60` everywhere, but `minutes_to_seconds(5)` is self-documenting. Anyone reading your code instantly knows you're converting 5 minutes to seconds — no mental math required.

